  "paper": "special-relativity",
  "argument": "arg-sr-doppler-aberration",
  "title": "Aberration: the direction changes too",
  "spoken": "The aberration direction cosine is cosine phi minus v over c, divided by one minus v over c times cosine phi.",
  "explanation": "The same substitution that gives the frequency ratio gives the light's direction in the moving system. At v = 0.6c, light crossing the motion at 90 degrees in the stationary system has cos phi′ = −0.6 in the moving one.",
  "review": "draft",
  "notation": "modern-pedagogical",
  "unitSystem": "si",
  "assumptions": [
    "The moving system moves along x at speed v relative to the stationary system, with |v| < c. Modern teaching notation: gamma is the paper's beta, c its V.",
    "A plane light wave whose direction makes the angle phi with the direction of motion, in the stationary system.",
    "The same wave phase, written in both systems' coordinates, fixes frequency and direction together."
  ],
